WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.470 --> 00:00:04.040
The pandemic has affected everyone

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:05.670
in different ways.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.950
Some pass the time by baking.

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:10.050
Homemade sourdough bread.

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:11.850
Do I need to say more?

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:14.310
Some chose to binge-watch Netflix.

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:15.263
It's your game.

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:19.580
Or maybe you started a garden oasis

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:20.981
in your backyard.

00:00:20.981 --> 00:00:23.257
Next I'm going to put my lettuces in.

00:00:23.257 --> 00:00:24.870
But for millions of others...

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:29.120
I'm going to be posting stocks
that will [expletive] boom.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.500
They wanted to uproot
the financial industry.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.750
Let's just stock it on
up and take $15,000.

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:36.400
Here's a stock that can change your life.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.160
I see a stock going up and I buy it

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.530
and I just watch it
until it stops going up.

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:41.363
And then I sell it.

00:00:41.363 --> 00:00:43.820
I have gained over $3 million.

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:45.510
I want some free money.

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:49.030
I don't think you can overstate
the online communities

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:51.000
of social media's influence

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.710
on financial markets.

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:55.700
A large group of inexperienced
investors who discussed

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:58.250
their plans on social
media platform Reddit

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:01.300
have shaken up the stock
market in a big way.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:02.750
The stock that everybody's talking about

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:04.200
right now is GameStop.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:05.860
Video game retailer GameStop

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:08.690
has set to continue their
head-spinning ascent today.

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:10.030
The revolutionaries on Reddit

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:12.340
are spanking Wall Street's ass.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:13.610
The more these stocks go up,

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:14.900
the more the big guys

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:17.450
are getting creamed and
losing billions of dollars.

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:19.900
This isn't a wealthy
person's game anymore.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:21.240
Anyone can play it.

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:23.060
I took up a little bit of a new hobby

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:25.900
and I am interested in day trading.

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:27.640
As an investor nowadays,

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:29.990
you don't have to look a specific way.

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:32.020
You don't have to wear specific clothes.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:34.270
You don't have to be doing
it from a trading floor

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:35.160
on Wall Street,

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:38.340
in a high-rise building
in Downtown Manhattan.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:41.190
The narrative of what's
driving the markets

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:43.690
is no longer being held

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:46.450
in secret offices in New York and Boston

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:49.370
of privileged people talking
to other privileged people.

00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:50.930
It's right out in the open.

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:51.910
It's on Twitter,

00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:53.230
it's on Reddit.

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:55.440
From brokerage apps to Discord channels

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.859
and subreddits to TikTok finfluencers.

00:01:57.859 --> 00:02:01.080
One of the last holdouts
in the world of disruption,

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:03.144
finance, is next in line.

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:16.893
Investing should be as
ubiquitous as shopping online.

00:02:18.155 --> 00:02:20.370
That's Vlad Tenev, the CEO of Robinhood

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:22.010
the online brokerage app,

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:24.020
talking about how he wants his app

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:26.050
to disrupt the world of stock trading

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:28.913
like Amazon disrupted the
world of home shopping.

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:32.300
And I think that speaks to exactly

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:35.310
the mission that a platform
like Robinhood has,

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:38.290
which is to democratize investing.

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:40.200
Robinhood was started in 2013

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.360
by Vlad Tenev and Baiju Bhatt

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:43.390
in, where else,

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:44.660
Silicon Valley,

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:47.030
as an alternative to the big brokerages,

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:49.400
but with one big difference.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:52.200
As they were building this social network

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:53.740
around finances for people,

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:56.120
they realized that there
was a gap in the market

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:57.809
and that free trading

00:02:57.809 --> 00:02:59.350
could become something

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:01.580
that they could bring to the markets.

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:03.273
They could disrupt the industry.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.210
While no-fee trading is industry standard

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:13.300
today, it was unheard of
when it was first introduced.

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.900
The online brokerage industry was really

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:19.210
innovative when they came on
the scene 10, 15 years ago

00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:20.830
but they kind of stopped innovating,

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:22.850
where you haven't seen
those commissions come

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:24.763
down beyond five or six or $7.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:30.230
Robinhood think that by
offering zero commission

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:32.860
that they're going to push
people onto this platform.

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:36.080
And on top of no-fee
trading, the customers

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:37.040
Robinhood was courting

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:40.130
have been long-overlooked
in the world of finance.

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:43.630
So the typical customer of a
Charles Schwab, for example,

00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:46.720
is putting a lot more money
in that brokerage account

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:48.730
than the typical customer at Robinhood.

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:50.787
Even today, with all of its popularity

00:03:50.787 --> 00:03:53.200
the median account size is about $240.

00:03:54.994 --> 00:03:56.547
The Fidelitys and the Schwabs

00:03:56.547 --> 00:03:59.200
and the E*TRADEs of the world,

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:01.660
they look down their nose
at those kind of accounts.

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:04.147
I don't want a $1000 account.

00:04:04.147 --> 00:04:05.070
They're just a headache.

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:07.110
I want a $100,000 account.

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:08.630
And this is key here, right?

00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:11.510
Because the thing about
Robinhood that's different

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:13.250
than every other single brokerage platform

00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:16.233
is how easy it is to use.

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:20.770
The design of the Robinhood app,

00:04:20.770 --> 00:04:23.080
really has in mind a user

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:25.933
who might be coming at
this absolutely cold.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.610
The fonts are very well-sized.

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:31.710
The shapes, the colors, all of that

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:33.800
is something that the
founders of Robinhood

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:36.470
paid really close attention to.

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:38.900
And by creating an interface that catered

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:41.420
to a newer investor, they were able to

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:43.693
gather a pretty healthy
group of customers.

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:48.423
And by 2019, they had
about 10 million customers.

00:04:51.359 --> 00:04:52.710
Those 10 million customers were

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:55.240
a huge success story for Robinhood,

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:57.540
but no one could have
predicted its explosion

00:04:57.540 --> 00:04:59.600
after the arrival of COVID-19.

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:02.830
Remain indoors, to the greatest extent.

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:04.770
And 100%

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:05.603
of the

00:05:05.603 --> 00:05:09.143
non-essential workforce must stay home.

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:13.870
What we saw when the
pandemic hit was the growth

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:17.560
of Robinhood users
absolutely turbocharged.

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:22.560
And oddly, you saw this surge
in interest in stock trading.

00:05:22.690 --> 00:05:27.520
Then they saw the absolute surge
in 2020 in customer growth.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:29.460
And now, by many accounts, they have more

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:31.190
than 20 million customers,

00:05:31.190 --> 00:05:33.630
which is pretty extraordinary.

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:37.510
So the next step for them is to figure out

00:05:37.510 --> 00:05:40.600
how to monetize those users

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:43.130
and how, even with their
small account sizes,

00:05:43.130 --> 00:05:45.100
to get them to grow with the company

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:49.313
and, as it releases more
products, come along with them.

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:55.700
Robinhood would become one of
the COVID economy's breakout

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:58.740
successes. Robinhood
traders, as they're called,

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:01.390
became the shorthand
explanation for the frenzy

00:06:01.390 --> 00:06:04.000
of often speculative retail investing

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:05.723
after the pandemic lockdowns.

00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:08.450
Kids wake up in the morning,

00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:09.550
they go on Robinhood.

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:11.120
They're in the shower,

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:12.910
they check Robinhood.

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:16.630
Kids are learning how the
stock market works at 18,

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:20.090
while they're at school,
checking their phones

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:22.980
between classes, because
notifications pop up

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:26.720
telling them what their
stocks are doing that day.

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:29.350
With these new tools,
Millennials and Gen Z

00:06:29.350 --> 00:06:31.430
are investing in the market in ways

00:06:31.430 --> 00:06:34.400
their parents could've
never even dreamt of.

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:37.070
That is key when we talk about

00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:39.820
the divide between older
and younger generations.

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:42.470
Is that the younger generation was exposed

00:06:42.470 --> 00:06:45.790
to so much more information and tools

00:06:45.790 --> 00:06:49.600
at their disposal, to do
what they want in the market.

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:52.250
The older generations, you
never had the technology

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:55.130
or the cost structure that
you could play the markets,

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:56.110
like you can today.

00:06:56.110 --> 00:06:58.660
So you were stuck with giving your money

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:02.456
to a professional investor and
paying them a fee to do it.

00:07:02.456 --> 00:07:04.337
When I speak to young kids, they tell me,

00:07:04.337 --> 00:07:05.640
"I'm making mistakes.

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:06.473
I'm losing money.

00:07:06.473 --> 00:07:09.410
I'm not a perfect investor, but I'm 18.

00:07:09.410 --> 00:07:12.283
And by the time I'm 30, which
will be 10 years from now,

00:07:12.283 --> 00:07:14.790
I'm going to have
interacted with the markets

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:15.730
for a decade."

00:07:15.730 --> 00:07:17.650
And so, it's a learning experience,

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:19.410
more so than anything else.

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:22.088
And you can look at it
and you can say, wow,

00:07:22.088 --> 00:07:22.921
that's risk-taking, that's dumb.

00:07:22.921 --> 00:07:25.150
But if you could possibly shave

00:07:25.150 --> 00:07:27.770
10, 20 years off your working life

00:07:27.770 --> 00:07:29.843
versus adding five years,

00:07:30.687 --> 00:07:33.233
I don't think you can
fault people for that.

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:37.780
But Robinhood is only part of the puzzle.

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:39.430
Sure. You can trade on it.

00:07:39.430 --> 00:07:42.510
But without research,
information and data, you're just

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:44.890
blindly throwing darts at different stocks

00:07:44.890 --> 00:07:47.050
and hoping to strike it rich.

00:07:47.050 --> 00:07:51.130
Wall Street, forever, has
always run on what we now

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:53.152
call narratives or memes,

00:07:53.152 --> 00:07:55.850
people telling rumors to each other,

00:07:55.850 --> 00:07:58.310
have been around for 100 years.

00:07:58.310 --> 00:07:59.280
On Wall Street,

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:01.780
information is its own currency.

00:08:01.780 --> 00:08:04.467
Historically, either you
had it or you didn't.

00:08:04.467 --> 00:08:08.040
And the people who had
it, had a huge advantage.

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:10.480
In the past, those narratives and memes

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:12.020
didn't have a network effect.

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:14.500
It would be one person
telling another person,

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:16.010
over the phone.

00:08:16.010 --> 00:08:18.380
In fact, when I broke into
this business in the eighties

00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:20.920
a lot of people used to
complain that they weren't

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:23.827
on the call list to get those ideas.

00:08:23.827 --> 00:08:26.360
[Narrator] Social media
changed all of that.

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:28.243
Fast forward to 2021.

00:08:28.243 --> 00:08:30.430
That's being done on the internet now.

00:08:30.430 --> 00:08:32.887
And that's being done
on Discord and Twitter

00:08:32.887 --> 00:08:35.660
and Stocktwits and Reddit.

00:08:35.660 --> 00:08:38.450
And the impact is very similar,

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:40.850
but orders and orders of magnitude larger.

00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:43.290
As social media has played
a bigger part in our lives,

00:08:43.290 --> 00:08:47.230
it's also played a bigger and
kind of more important role

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:50.920
in how people find investing ideas online

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:52.890
and on these platforms.

00:08:52.890 --> 00:08:54.700
How do those ideas spread?

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:56.010
On TikTok

00:08:56.010 --> 00:08:57.817
Yes, that TikTok.

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:06.847
People aren't just going to
it for sea shanty videos.

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:10.030
The #PersonalFinance hashtag

00:09:10.030 --> 00:09:13.250
on TikTok has garnered 3.5 billion views

00:09:13.250 --> 00:09:16.100
from its 1 billion monthly active users.

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:18.350
By comparison, videos found under

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:20.520
the #Cooking and #HealthTips hashtag

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:24.760
have 2.6 and 2.1 billion
views, respectively.

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:27.070
The trajectory of Robinhood and Tiktok

00:09:27.070 --> 00:09:29.830
were literally on par.

00:09:29.830 --> 00:09:31.710
What happened in the
stock market this week?

00:09:31.710 --> 00:09:34.220
So the word of the week is "expectations".

00:09:34.220 --> 00:09:35.530
It's absolutely essential that you guys

00:09:35.530 --> 00:09:37.810
understand the risks of shorting.

00:09:37.810 --> 00:09:40.500
And Kayla Kilbride and
Kyla Scanlon are two

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:44.010
of those financial influencer
TikTokers, also known

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:45.510
as "finfluencers".

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:47.100
I think people still care about fashion.

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:48.710
They care about culture,
but it's also like,

00:09:48.710 --> 00:09:51.490
there's this big behemoth
thing called the stock market

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:53.825
that we really don't
know a whole lot about.

00:09:53.825 --> 00:09:55.390
And it's gatekept behind industry.

00:09:55.390 --> 00:09:57.398
And so people want to unpack that.

00:09:57.398 --> 00:10:00.060
And so my audiences, a lot of them are

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:03.263
younger investors who are
looking to hone their skills.

00:10:03.263 --> 00:10:04.916
Ticking right up.

00:10:04.916 --> 00:10:07.530
Kayla and Kayla are two of the more,

00:10:07.530 --> 00:10:10.760
let's say, professional
TikTok influencers.

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:14.110
The Tesla ARK Bitcoin
Biotech risk cluster.

00:10:14.110 --> 00:10:15.920
I have been trading since high school

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:18.440
and it was a really interesting way for me

00:10:18.440 --> 00:10:20.500
to fill out my decision-making frameworks

00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:21.950
and to think about thinking.

00:10:21.950 --> 00:10:23.150
And I think that the more that

00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:24.900
we can have people thinking about finance,

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:26.987
the better that we're going
to progress as a society.

00:10:26.987 --> 00:10:30.590
I think it's a huge gap
in our educational system.

00:10:30.590 --> 00:10:31.950
If you're going to buy
an option for a stock

00:10:31.950 --> 00:10:33.970
you think is going to go up, it's a call.

00:10:33.970 --> 00:10:35.060
So think of it like this.

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:36.827
I made the options video where I was like,

00:10:36.827 --> 00:10:39.390
"Options are just like shopping".

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:40.520
Rumor has it that this palette's

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:41.420
about to go up in price.

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:45.580
So if I come in tomorrow, can
I still buy it at said price?

00:10:45.580 --> 00:10:49.370
And a ton of women just
attacked that video

00:10:49.370 --> 00:10:52.400
and were like, "You explained
things so much better than

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:54.890
my boyfriend or my professor,"

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:57.050
or "I could sit and
listen to you all day."

00:10:57.050 --> 00:11:01.327
And I was like, "This is
what I was looking for."

00:11:01.327 --> 00:11:02.250
But TikTok's algorithm

00:11:02.250 --> 00:11:04.640
does not reward calm and measured videos

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:06.630
about responsible investing.

00:11:06.630 --> 00:11:07.940
It rewards this.

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:08.773
Watch this video

00:11:08.773 --> 00:11:10.280
for 30 seconds and you'll be rich for

00:11:10.280 --> 00:11:11.620
the rest of your life.

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:14.350
I try not to say the A
word, the A-L-G-O word,

00:11:14.350 --> 00:11:17.738
because TikTok listens to it.

00:11:17.738 --> 00:11:20.287
The algorithm does impact how you feel.

00:11:20.287 --> 00:11:22.610
You do notice which videos do better

00:11:22.610 --> 00:11:24.490
versus which videos do worse.

00:11:24.490 --> 00:11:25.930
How do you catch someone's attention

00:11:25.930 --> 00:11:27.700
in less than 60 seconds?

00:11:27.700 --> 00:11:29.037
And how do you make it
something that they're like,

00:11:29.037 --> 00:11:30.540
"I need this in my life"?

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:32.810
Nearly impossible to
do that in 60 seconds.

00:11:32.810 --> 00:11:34.197
But the people who perfect it,

00:11:34.197 --> 00:11:35.600
are the people that you're going to watch

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:37.250
and are going to stick around.

00:11:37.250 --> 00:11:39.810
And where people are
sticking around, Reddit.

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:42.843
And it's nearly countless
subreddits about finance.

00:11:45.503 --> 00:11:49.010
And by far, the biggest one
of those is wallstreetbets.

00:11:49.010 --> 00:11:51.610
There's a lot of social
media platforms out there,

00:11:51.610 --> 00:11:55.150
but Reddit is the social media

00:11:55.150 --> 00:11:57.160
platform for finance.

00:11:57.160 --> 00:12:01.020
Places like wallstreetbets
is where it all goes down.

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:02.950
Started in January, 2012,

00:12:02.950 --> 00:12:05.180
it wasn't long before
wallstreetbets adopted

00:12:05.180 --> 00:12:08.377
a 4chan-like etiquette,
leading to their tagline,

00:12:08.377 --> 00:12:11.080
"Like 4han found a Bloomberg Terminal".

00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:13.030
We had all these unexplained stock moves

00:12:13.030 --> 00:12:16.600
and we would be grasping for why stock XYZ

00:12:16.600 --> 00:12:18.410
jumped 20% in a day.

00:12:18.410 --> 00:12:19.667
And you would look at wallstreetbets

00:12:19.667 --> 00:12:21.200
and you would see people there talking

00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:23.190
about buying options in that stock

00:12:23.190 --> 00:12:26.720
and it sort of squared
the circle, so to speak,

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:28.130
and started making sense.

00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:29.460
And it was at that point that it was like,

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:31.900
wow, we have to start
paying attention to this.

00:12:31.900 --> 00:12:36.080
Some people on the platform
really spend tons of time.

00:12:36.080 --> 00:12:38.050
And others are just
looking for entertainment

00:12:38.050 --> 00:12:40.380
and to have a little bit of fun.

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:43.620
Effectively gambling or
looking for an outlet,

00:12:43.620 --> 00:12:45.740
trying to make a little bit of money.

00:12:45.740 --> 00:12:48.290
Then on top of all of that,
there were stimulus checks.

00:12:48.290 --> 00:12:50.110
Now, clearly this money's going to people

00:12:50.110 --> 00:12:52.490
who need to use that to eat, to live.

00:12:52.490 --> 00:12:53.430
But there's a lot of people

00:12:53.430 --> 00:12:56.659
who used this money to
invest in the stock market.

00:12:57.580 --> 00:12:59.270
And it's not just stock tips.

00:12:59.270 --> 00:13:01.406
People quote, unquote, YOLO
their entire life savings

00:13:01.406 --> 00:13:05.613
or stimulus checks on a single stock.

00:13:05.613 --> 00:13:07.880
They post what is called
gain and loss porn,

00:13:07.880 --> 00:13:10.860
screen-grabbing staggering
hits and misses on stocks

00:13:10.860 --> 00:13:12.880
for the entire community to see.

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:14.830
I am about to lose everything.

00:13:16.847 --> 00:13:19.270
And while some of the
people on wallstreetbets

00:13:19.270 --> 00:13:23.330
are novice investors, the
community contains multitudes.

00:13:23.330 --> 00:13:25.850
In one respect, what it did show us,

00:13:25.850 --> 00:13:29.390
among these network groups is,
they are very sophisticated.

00:13:29.390 --> 00:13:31.850
They're not a bunch of ignorant people

00:13:31.850 --> 00:13:34.010
talking to other ignorant people.

00:13:34.010 --> 00:13:35.250
And this is where the pieces

00:13:35.250 --> 00:13:37.690
of the puzzle all come
together to wreak havoc

00:13:37.690 --> 00:13:41.680
on Wall Street for a
solid week in early 2021.

00:13:41.680 --> 00:13:44.270
It's pretty clear that the combination

00:13:44.270 --> 00:13:46.850
of more people having
retail brokerage accounts

00:13:46.850 --> 00:13:50.090
and trading apps and more
people being stuck at home

00:13:50.090 --> 00:13:51.680
looking for some kind of entertainment

00:13:51.680 --> 00:13:53.640
and some kind of community online,

00:13:53.640 --> 00:13:56.493
came together in this very singular way.

00:13:57.820 --> 00:13:59.973
What came together is GameStop.

00:14:03.350 --> 00:14:05.650
Let's go back to the
beginnings of what happened

00:14:05.650 --> 00:14:07.860
with GameStop, a couple of years ago.

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:10.480
On the boards like Reddit and Stocktwits,

00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:12.000
a lot of people were talking

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:15.520
about GameStop as being attractive value.

00:14:15.520 --> 00:14:19.030
Some of them are even Wall Street alumns,

00:14:19.030 --> 00:14:22.360
who left the industry and now
still have access to tools

00:14:22.360 --> 00:14:25.420
that they can use to improve
their financial wellbeing

00:14:25.420 --> 00:14:27.260
and make very good decisions.

00:14:27.260 --> 00:14:30.622
And I think Roaring Kitty is
a perfect example of that.

00:14:30.622 --> 00:14:31.800
GameStop is one of the most compelling

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:33.650
asymmetric opportunities
in the market today.

00:14:33.650 --> 00:14:35.470
Really, I don't understand how
you can disagree with that.

00:14:35.470 --> 00:14:39.152
At the same time that this
crowd was talking it up,

00:14:39.152 --> 00:14:42.030
the hedge fund institutional crowd

00:14:42.030 --> 00:14:43.720
didn't like this stock at all.

00:14:43.720 --> 00:14:47.000
And it had the largest
short interest of any stock

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:50.480
in the New York Stock
Exchange going into late 2020.

00:14:50.480 --> 00:14:51.840
Short selling is a bet

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:53.500
that a stock price will fall

00:14:53.500 --> 00:14:55.940
and it's a fairly simple concept.

00:14:55.940 --> 00:14:58.600
An investor borrows a
stock and then sells it.

00:14:58.600 --> 00:15:01.500
The investor is betting on
buying the stock back later

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:03.720
for a lower price, before returning it

00:15:03.720 --> 00:15:07.270
to the original owner,
pocketing the price difference.

00:15:07.270 --> 00:15:09.660
What a short seller would tell you is that

00:15:09.660 --> 00:15:13.900
they almost act as a
policeman in financial markets

00:15:13.900 --> 00:15:15.240
or they're, at least, looking

00:15:15.240 --> 00:15:18.400
for companies that aren't
what they say they are.

00:15:18.400 --> 00:15:22.350
And that can range anywhere
from an outright fraud

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:24.610
that some short sellers try to expose

00:15:24.610 --> 00:15:26.770
to just a company that's not worth as much

00:15:26.770 --> 00:15:28.840
as investors think it is.

00:15:28.840 --> 00:15:31.240
The problem is, if the stock price rises,

00:15:31.240 --> 00:15:32.590
the short seller has to come up

00:15:32.590 --> 00:15:34.480
with the money to buy the stock back.

00:15:34.480 --> 00:15:36.820
And the more it rises, the more it costs.

00:15:36.820 --> 00:15:38.760
And that's what's called a short squeeze.

00:15:38.760 --> 00:15:40.770
And the hedge fund Melvin Capital was

00:15:40.770 --> 00:15:43.070
on the wrong end of that squeeze.

00:15:43.070 --> 00:15:46.569
Melvin Capital lost over 50% of its money

00:15:46.569 --> 00:15:48.970
in the month of January alone,

00:15:48.970 --> 00:15:52.120
just from being caught in the
short squeeze of this stock.

00:15:52.120 --> 00:15:54.122
This is where the disruption comes in.

00:15:55.960 --> 00:15:58.670
One of the most wild stories
you're ever going to see,

00:15:58.670 --> 00:16:00.960
GameStop shares absolutely going nuts.

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:02.840
In less than a year, the company's stock

00:16:02.840 --> 00:16:07.824
has jumped from less than
$3 a share to almost $350.

00:16:07.824 --> 00:16:11.010
Cheers, everybody!

00:16:11.010 --> 00:16:14.020
Roaring Kitty has turned
$50,000 or so investment

00:16:14.020 --> 00:16:16.768
into some $14 million.

00:16:16.768 --> 00:16:19.600
Wh-wh-whaaaa.

00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:22.250
Investors all over
social media use the free

00:16:22.250 --> 00:16:25.840
trading app Robinhood to
wreak havoc on Wall Street.

00:16:25.840 --> 00:16:29.040
They're actually piling on
into some of these stocks to

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:31.290
really hurt the
professional short sellers.

00:16:31.290 --> 00:16:33.540
And they all used Reddit, Twitter, TikTok

00:16:33.540 --> 00:16:36.250
and Discord to tell one
another to keep buying

00:16:36.250 --> 00:16:39.700
and quote, "hold the line"
and not sell GameStop.

00:16:39.700 --> 00:16:41.840
People are getting
cheerleaded for jumping in,

00:16:41.840 --> 00:16:44.140
buying at the highs,
they're saying keep going.

00:16:44.140 --> 00:16:45.750
And it was working too.

00:16:45.750 --> 00:16:46.720
Until...

00:16:48.940 --> 00:16:51.780
Thursday morning, the trading
app Robinhood sent out

00:16:51.780 --> 00:16:54.920
this Tweet, "In light of
current market volatility,

00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:58.730
we are restricting transactions,"
including GameStop.

00:16:58.730 --> 00:17:00.210
People were really angry.

00:17:00.210 --> 00:17:03.440
Robinhood was supposed to
allow the regular people

00:17:03.440 --> 00:17:07.020
to play the same game
these big boys are playing.

00:17:07.020 --> 00:17:09.340
But instead we were all taken for schmucks

00:17:09.340 --> 00:17:10.560
and they screwed us.

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:12.990
Shares of GameStop are jumping again,

00:17:12.990 --> 00:17:14.860
now that retail investors
are being allowed to

00:17:14.860 --> 00:17:16.330
buy shares of the stock.

00:17:16.330 --> 00:17:21.330
But what we learned, is that
when people organize online,

00:17:21.720 --> 00:17:25.290
they have the ability
to disrupt the market.

00:17:25.290 --> 00:17:30.280
And it was a big sign that
ivory walls of institution

00:17:30.280 --> 00:17:31.850
aren't as powerful as, maybe,

00:17:31.850 --> 00:17:33.960
the institutions thought them to be.

00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:36.410
I don't know if pumping up
GameStop was the best use

00:17:36.410 --> 00:17:38.880
of billions of dollars, but I think that,

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:40.730
as a signal to society,

00:17:40.730 --> 00:17:43.351
it was pretty interesting and powerful.

00:17:43.351 --> 00:17:45.810
The GameStop saga was so impactful

00:17:45.810 --> 00:17:48.160
that both Vlad Tenev
and Roaring Kitty were

00:17:48.160 --> 00:17:50.550
called in to testify before Congress

00:17:50.550 --> 00:17:53.570
to clarify each of
their roles in the saga.

00:17:53.570 --> 00:17:55.520
One thing that's emerged from the

00:17:55.520 --> 00:17:57.430
congressional hearings is that

00:17:57.430 --> 00:18:00.820
they're interested in how
retail brokerages make money

00:18:00.820 --> 00:18:03.660
and whether they're
keeping the best interests

00:18:03.660 --> 00:18:05.460
of their customers in mind.

00:18:05.460 --> 00:18:07.650
But it's pretty fuzzy, as far as,

00:18:07.650 --> 00:18:10.333
what they would actually do as a result.

00:18:16.460 --> 00:18:19.510
While the frenzy around
GameStop eventually subsided,

00:18:19.510 --> 00:18:21.770
it didn't take long
for retail investors to

00:18:21.770 --> 00:18:24.620
find other areas to invest their money.

00:18:24.620 --> 00:18:27.253
Take a look at Bitcoin going
bananas over the weekend.

00:18:27.253 --> 00:18:30.830
Bitcoin forms a brand-new, all-time-high.

00:18:30.830 --> 00:18:33.240
It was after GameStop started to drop that

00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:36.970
we saw Bitcoin really
go up to over 60,000.

00:18:36.970 --> 00:18:39.400
How much further does
this rally have to run?

00:18:39.400 --> 00:18:41.650
It's compelling to say that money shifted

00:18:41.650 --> 00:18:43.760
from one venue to the other.

00:18:43.760 --> 00:18:46.660
Well, correlation doesn't equal causation.

00:18:46.660 --> 00:18:49.620
There's a chart out there
showing an inverse correlation

00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:53.810
on meme stocks going down and
cryptocurrencies going up.

00:18:53.810 --> 00:18:57.000
There's definitely an aspect
where people are making money

00:18:57.000 --> 00:18:59.440
on stocks and then
moving... They're agnostic.

00:18:59.440 --> 00:19:00.460
They're not hedge funds.

00:19:00.460 --> 00:19:02.430
They can invest wherever
the hell they want.

00:19:02.430 --> 00:19:04.310
It's a classic sort of momentum trade,

00:19:04.310 --> 00:19:05.830
they're going to wherever the winners are

00:19:05.830 --> 00:19:08.763
and they're willing to
jump on and ride it.

00:19:12.630 --> 00:19:15.414
So whether it's meme stocks like GameStop,

00:19:15.414 --> 00:19:17.530
meme coins like Dogecoin,

00:19:17.530 --> 00:19:21.290
or entirely new ways to
invest in art like NFTs,

00:19:21.290 --> 00:19:23.330
the new kind of retail investor

00:19:23.330 --> 00:19:25.530
is making its presence known.

00:19:25.530 --> 00:19:28.920
Over the next 10, maybe 15 years,

00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:30.880
you're going to continue to see,

00:19:30.880 --> 00:19:32.910
whether by choice or by force,

00:19:32.910 --> 00:19:37.270
that baton go from older
generations to younger generations.

00:19:37.270 --> 00:19:38.906
I don't think any single app

00:19:38.906 --> 00:19:41.460
is going to be the thing that disrupts.

00:19:41.460 --> 00:19:43.960
I think people are the disruptors

00:19:43.960 --> 00:19:47.740
How people get ideas
about what to invest in

00:19:47.740 --> 00:19:51.360
has become increasingly
entwined with social media.

00:19:51.360 --> 00:19:54.630
And as social media companies

00:19:54.630 --> 00:19:56.960
govern more and more of our lives,

00:19:56.960 --> 00:19:59.510
the fact that now, they're tied

00:19:59.510 --> 00:20:01.660
in more intimately to people's wallets,

00:20:01.660 --> 00:20:05.200
is something that we might
want to understand better.

00:20:05.200 --> 00:20:07.781
Usually what disruption, I think, means

00:20:07.781 --> 00:20:10.070
is technology comes in,

00:20:10.070 --> 00:20:12.450
whether it's blogs to the newspapers,

00:20:12.450 --> 00:20:16.370
or online retailing to brick and mortar.

00:20:16.370 --> 00:20:18.520
They come in and they're
in the same business,

00:20:18.520 --> 00:20:22.920
but they just do it at such
a gigantic cost savings,

00:20:22.920 --> 00:20:25.420
that it causes everybody to switch.

00:20:25.420 --> 00:20:27.290
Well, that's the risk that happens here,

00:20:27.290 --> 00:20:32.140
is that it's so much cheaper
to just manage your own money.

00:20:32.140 --> 00:20:35.570
We're not there yet, but
costs and conveniences

00:20:35.570 --> 00:20:36.610
are moving in that direction.

00:20:36.610 --> 00:20:39.949
So I'd say Wall Street, you
haven't been disrupted yet

00:20:39.949 --> 00:20:43.310
but you could be in the coming years.

00:20:43.310 --> 00:20:44.410
And that the final chapter

00:20:44.410 --> 00:20:46.110
on this story hasn't been written.

